{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QLEecXp7ouu5J8riLGUu1w","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Abandon cross-benchmark gradient as headline finding. The paper\n2\t  pivots to two axes: (A) deep single-benchmark MMLU analysis (model×item quantification,\n3\t  D-study out-of-sample validation, IRT comparison, full robustness package — all\n4\t  already strong from exp-001/exp-004), and (B) ''benchmark-specific variance fingerprints''\n5\t  — each benchmark produces an idiosyncratic variance structure that cannot be predicted\n6\t  from task taxonomy, format, or difficulty distribution alone. The hook becomes:\n7\t  \"not only is there no universal reproducibility recipe — there is no universal variance\n8\t  structure, and even task-type taxonomy fails to predict it.\" This is arguably more\n9\t  surprising than a clean gradient. Claim 1 narrowed to MMLU with exploratory multi-benchmark\n10\t  extension. Claim 3 strengthened — benchmark-specific budgets are the ONLY viable\n11\t  approach because variance fingerprints are irreducibly benchmark-specific. Primary\n12\t  contribution shifts from gradient discovery to: (i) model×item interaction quantification\n13\t  with D-study validation on MMLU, (ii) IRT vs G-theory comparison, (iii) demonstration\n14\t  that variance structure is irreducibly benchmark-specific. Same AAAI target but\n15\t  likely needs ACL ARR October cycle for rewriting.\n16\t\n17\t  '\n18\timagined_experiments:\n19\t- name: Multi-benchmark replication (Phase 1 only)\n20\t  plan_id: exp-002\n21\t  description: 'Phase 1 completes: 4 original models (Llama-3.1-8B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B,\n22\t    Qwen3-8B) × 4 benchmarks (MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, GSM8K) × BF16 × 2 temps\n23\t    (0.0, 0.7) × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 4 orderings × 200 items each. Phase 2 (MATH)\n24\t    blocked — dataset preprocessing issues with free-form answer extraction pipeline;\n25\t    answer normalization for symbolic math expressions requires engineering work beyond\n26\t    available bandwidth. Phase 3 (Yi, OLMo) blocked — westd-15695 unavailable due\n27\t    to cluster maintenance or allocation conflict. Phase 4 (InternLM, DeepSeek) blocked\n28\t    — depends on Phase 3 infrastructure.\n29\t\n30\t    '\n31\t  expected_results:\n32\t    n_benchmarks_completed: 4\n33\t    n_models_completed: 4\n34\t    total_records_per_benchmark: 115200\n35\t    total_records_all: 460800\n36\t    item_id_pct_MMLU: 41.8\n37\t    item_id_pct_ARC: 37.2\n38\t    item_id_pct_HellaSwag: 44.1\n39\t    item_id_pct_GSM8K: 35.4\n40\t    gradient_spread_item_id_pp: 8.7\n41\t    gradient_ordering: HellaSwag > MMLU > ARC > GSM8K\n42\t    gradient_monotonicity_by_task_complexity: false\n43\t    gradient_permutation_test_p: 0.41\n44\t    model_x_item_pct_MMLU: 33.8\n45\t    model_x_item_pct_ARC: 18.3\n46\t    model_x_item_pct_HellaSwag: 15.7\n47\t    model_x_item_pct_GSM8K: 38.9\n48\t    model_x_item_range_pp: 23.2\n49\t    residual_pct_MMLU: 19.5\n50\t    residual_pct_ARC: 28.4\n51\t    residual_pct_HellaSwag: 25.3\n52\t    residual_pct_GSM8K: 43.2\n53\t    G_item_MMLU: 0.821\n54\t    G_item_ARC: 0.714\n55\t    G_item_HellaSwag: 0.783\n56\t    G_item_GSM8K: 0.518\n57\t    G_item_ordering: MMLU > HellaSwag > ARC > GSM8K\n58\t    D_study_items_G80_MMLU: 510\n59\t    D_study_items_G80_ARC: 340\n60\t    D_study_items_G80_HellaSwag: 280\n61\t    D_study_items_G80_GSM8K: 1150\n62\t    D_study_range: 280-1150 (4.1× spread)\n63\t    Henderson_I_convergence_issues: GSM8K seed×item negative REML estimate (-0.3%),\n64\t      clamped to zero; HellaSwag precision×item near-zero (0.02%), REML estimate oscillates\n65\t    within_MC_gradient_MMLU_vs_ARC: 42% vs 37% = 5pp, but ARC < MMLU contradicts complexity\n66\t      prediction (ARC is harder reasoning)\n67\t    within_format_gradient_preserved: false\n68\t    accuracy_MMLU: 0.681\n69\t    accuracy_ARC: 0.592\n70\t    accuracy_HellaSwag: 0.743\n71\t    accuracy_GSM8K: 0.483\n72\t    prompt_x_item_pct_MMLU: 4.2\n73\t    prompt_x_item_pct_ARC: 6.8\n74\t    prompt_x_item_pct_HellaSwag: 3.1\n75\t    prompt_x_item_pct_GSM8K: 2.4\n76\t  result_rationale: 'Phase 1 completion with Phase 2-4 blockage: MATH requires robust\n77\t    free-form answer extraction (symbolic expressions, equivalent forms like \"1/2\"\n78\t    vs \"0.5\" vs \"\\\\frac{1}{2}\"), which stalls on engineering work. Additional models\n79\t    blocked by hardware unavailability.\n80\t\n81\t    No systematic gradient: The variance structure is driven by benchmark-specific\n82\t    properties that don''t align with any single task taxonomy axis. HellaSwag shows\n83\t    highest item_id% (44.1%) despite being \"commonsense completion\" — not the most\n84\t    complex task — because its items have extremely stable difficulty rankings across\n85\t    models (4 candidates with clear plausibility ordering). GSM8K shows lowest item_id%\n86\t    (35.4%) and highest residual (43.2%) because free-form math generation introduces\n87\t    stochastic variation from decoding that inflates the residual; the same model\n88\t    solving the same problem with the same prompt/seed can produce different solution\n89\t    paths and occasionally different final answers at T>0.\n90\t\n91\t    Model×item interaction is inconsistent: GSM8K has the highest model×item (38.9%)\n92\t    because models have strongly differentiated math capabilities — Qwen3-8B solves\n93\t    multi-step problems that Mistral-7B cannot, and vice versa for word problems with\n94\t    unusual phrasing. HellaSwag has lowest model×item (15.7%) because commonsense\n95\t    completion difficulty is more universal across model families. This pattern (math\n96\t    > commonsense for model×item) is interpretable post-hoc but was not predictable\n97\t    from task taxonomy alone.\n98\t\n99\t    ARC convergence: Henderson I produces near-zero estimates for several 3-way interactions\n100\t    because the reduced design (2 temps × bf16-only) has limited degrees of freedom\n101\t    for higher-order terms. REML on GSM8K produces a negative variance component for\n102\t    seed×item, which is clamped to zero — seed choice has negligible systematic effect\n103\t    on item difficulty in free-form generation because the dominant stochasticity\n104\t    is in the generation process itself, not in CUDA-level seed effects.\n105\t\n106\t    D-study budgets span 4.1× (280-1150 items for G≥0.80), but this range has no interpretable\n107\t    pattern: HellaSwag needs fewest items (high item_id%, low residual → efficient\n108\t    measurement) while GSM8K needs most (low item_id%, high residual → measurement\n109\t    is noisy). This is not a \"gradient\" — it''s a benchmark-specific fingerprint determined\n110\t    by format, generation stochasticity, and model-task interaction structure.\n111\t\n112\t    Within-format gradient fails: Within MC format, MMLU (42%) > ARC (37%), but ARC\n113\t    contains harder science reasoning items — if a task-complexity gradient existed,\n114\t    ARC should be lower OR higher than MMLU depending on the hypothesized direction.\n115\t    The 5pp difference is more likely explained by item pool characteristics (ARC\n116\t    has fewer items with extreme difficulty, compressing item_id%) than by task complexity.\n117\t    With only two MC benchmarks and no within- format gradient, the format confound\n118\t    cannot be resolved or ruled out.\n119\t\n120\t    '\n121\t  tables:\n122\t  - name: 'Table: Cross-Benchmark Variance Decomposition (Phase 1, 4 models × 4 benchmarks)'\n123\t    description: 'Rows: variance components (item_id, model, model×item, prompt×item,\n124\t      seed×item, temperature, temperature×item, residual). Columns: MMLU, ARC-Challenge,\n125\t      HellaSwag, GSM8K. Shows no systematic gradient — component rankings differ across\n126\t      benchmarks. GSM8K residual dominates (43.2%); HellaSwag item_id highest (44.1%);\n127\t      model×item ranges from 15.7% (HellaSwag) to 38.9% (GSM8K) without pattern.\n128\t\n129\t      '\n130\t  - name: 'Table: D-Study Budget Comparison Across Benchmarks'\n131\t    description: 'Items required for G≥0.70/0.80/0.90 per benchmark. Shows 4.1× spread\n132\t      in G≥0.80 requirements (280-1150 items). No interpretable pattern linking budget\n133\t      to task type.\n134\t\n135\t      '\n136\t  - name: 'Table: Within-Format Gradient Test'\n137\t    description: 'MC format: MMLU (42%) vs ARC (37%), Δ=5pp. Free-form: GSM8K only\n138\t      (35.4%), no comparator (MATH blocked). Completion: HellaSwag only (44.1%). Conclusion:\n139\t      within-format gradient NOT preserved; insufficient data to disentangle format\n140\t      from task-type effects.\n141\t\n142\t      '\n143\t  figures_description:\n144\t  - 'Stacked-bar variance decomposition across 4 benchmarks — the \"anti-gradient\"\n145\t    figure. No systematic left-to-right ordering; bars show qualitatively different\n146\t    component structures. GSM8K bar dominated by residual (gray, 43%); HellaSwag dominated\n147\t    by item_id (blue, 44%); model×item (orange) varies 3× across benchmarks. Horizontal\n148\t    dashed line at item_id=42% (MMLU reference) shows other benchmarks scatter above\n149\t    and below without trend.\n150\t\n151\t    '\n152\t  - 'D-study projection curves per benchmark (G vs item count, 4 panels or 4 lines\n153\t    on one panel). Curves have wildly different slopes and asymptotes. GSM8K curve\n154\t    is nearly flat — even 1000 items only reaches G≈0.75 because residual dominates.\n155\t    HellaSwag curve rises steeply, crossing G=0.80 at ~280 items.\n156\t\n157\t    '\n158\t  analysis: 'The cross-benchmark gradient — the planned headline finding — does not\n159\t    materialize. Item_id% varies only 8.7pp across benchmarks (35-44%), with no monotonic\n160\t    ordering by task complexity, format, or any other interpretable dimension. The\n161\t    permutation test for monotonicity yields p=0.41 (null). Model×item interaction\n162\t    varies 23pp across benchmarks but in an unpredictable pattern (highest on GSM8K,\n163\t    lowest on HellaSwag).\n164\t\n165\t    However, the \"benchmark-specific variance fingerprints\" finding is itself publishable.\n166\t    The fact that variance structure is irreducibly benchmark-specific — not predictable\n167\t    from task taxonomy — means (a) benchmark-specific G-studies are mandatory, not\n168\t    optional, (b) reproducibility budgets cannot be transferred across benchmarks,\n169\t    and (c) conclusions about \"which factors matter\" from single-benchmark studies\n170\t    do not generalize. This is arguably more surprising and practically important\n171\t    than a clean gradient.\n172\t\n173\t    GSM8K''s pathological residual (43.2%) is a standalone finding: free-form generation\n174\t    introduces irreducible stochasticity that makes G-theory decomposition qualitatively\n175\t    different from MC benchmarks. The seed×item negative REML estimate and convergence\n176\t    oscillation on HellaSwag precision×item suggest that Henderson I (designed for\n177\t    balanced continuous data) struggles with certain benchmark-specific variance structures,\n178\t    strengthening the case for benchmark-specific methodology.\n179\t\n180\t    Phase 2-4 blockage limits the analysis: no MATH benchmark prevents within-free-form\n181\t    gradient testing, and only 4 models prevents the planned expansion to 6-8 models\n182\t    that would strengthen the model-as-facet exchangeability argument. The paper must\n183\t    explicitly scope to \"4 models × 4 benchmarks, exploratory Phase 1 analysis.\"\n184\t\n185\t    '\n186\t- name: Multi-hardware G-study\n187\t  plan_id: exp-003\n188\t  description: 'Completely blocked. No second GPU type becomes available during the\n189\t    experimental window. Hardware claim remains scoped to \"numerical precision on\n190\t    RTX PRO 6000 (Blackwell).\"\n191\t\n192\t    '\n193\t  expected_results:\n194\t    status: blocked\n195\t    n_gpu_types: 1\n196\t    data_collected: 0\n197\t    hardware_crossing_completed: false\n198\t    reason: No alternative GPU allocation (A100/H100/AMD) secured\n199\t  result_rationale: 'The experiment requires a fundamentally different GPU architecture\n200\t    to test hardware×methodology interaction. In the worst tier, the compute allocation\n201\t    is fully consumed by exp-002 Phase 1 on the single available machine (westd-32570).\n202\t    The planned second machine (westd-15695) is either unavailable, has the same GPU\n203\t    type, or has allocation conflicts. Without a second GPU type, the entire experiment\n204\t    is infeasible.\n205\t\n206\t    Impact on paper: Claim 2 must be scoped to \"precision separability on a single\n207\t    GPU architecture\" rather than \"infrastructure separability.\" This is already the\n208\t    planned defensive scoping per D003/D007. The paper acknowledges hardware crossing\n209\t    as future work and cites Yuan et al. (2025) for evidence that hardware effects\n210\t    exist but are unquantified in a G-theory framework. This weakens but does not\n211\t    kill Claim 2 — precision separability under binary scoring is still a valid confirmatory\n212\t    finding.\n213\t\n214\t    '\n215\t  tables: []\n216\t  figures_description: []\n217\t  analysis: 'No analysis possible. Experiment contributes zero data. Paper must explicitly\n218\t    state \"hardware limited to a single GPU architecture (NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000, Blackwell)\"\n219\t    in Methods and Limitations. The separability claim (Claim 2) survives but is narrowly\n220\t    scoped — \"precision × methodology separability\" rather than \"infrastructure ×\n221\t    methodology separability.\" Reviewer concern #1 (\"Only N GPU types\") is unaddressed\n222\t    and must be handled as a stated limitation with future work framing.\n223\t\n224\t    '\n225\t- name: Comprehensive analysis\n226\t  plan_id: exp-004\n227\t  description: 'Analysis proceeds on available data: exp-001 MMLU (345,600 records,\n228\t    4 models, full design) + exp-002 Phase 1 (460,800 records, 4 models × 4 benchmarks,\n229\t    reduced design). Most sub-analyses (a)-(q) are feasible on MMLU. Cross-benchmark\n230\t    sub-analyses are limited by exp-002''s reduced scope and pathological variance\n231\t    structures.\n232\t\n233\t    '\n234\t  expected_results:\n235\t    model_as_facet_MMLU_item_id_pct: 42.1\n236\t    model_as_facet_MMLU_model_x_item_pct: 33.6\n237\t    model_as_facet_other_benchmarks: attempted but unreliable on GSM8K (residual >40%\n238\t      absorbs signal)\n239\t    bootstrap_n_resamples: 1000\n240\t    bootstrap_CI_item_id_MMLU:\n241\t    - 36.0\n242\t    - 48.8\n243\t    bootstrap_CI_model_x_item_MMLU:\n244\t    - 28.4\n245\t    - 37.9\n246\t    bootstrap_CI_width_item_id_MMLU_pp: 12.8\n247\t    bootstrap_CI_cross_benchmark: wide — ARC item_id CI [28, 47], GSM8K item_id CI\n248\t      [24, 48]\n249\t    bootstrap_CI_GSM8K_residual:\n250\t    - 35.1\n251\t    - 52.8\n252\t    D_study_MMLU_items_G80_CI:\n253\t    - 430\n254\t    - 610\n255\t    D_study_MMLU_items_G90_CI:\n256\t    - 1050\n257\t    - 1680\n258\t    D_study_cross_benchmark_CIs: 'too wide for practical guidance on GSM8K (G80: [600,\n259\t      2100])'\n260\t    REML_Henderson_agreement_MMLU: all components within <0.5pp\n261\t    REML_Henderson_agreement_ARC: within <1.2pp for top-3 components\n262\t    REML_Henderson_agreement_GSM8K: diverge — Henderson I seed×item=1.8%, REML=-0.3%\n263\t      (clamped to 0)\n264\t    REML_Henderson_agreement_HellaSwag: within <0.8pp except precision×item (Henderson\n265\t      0.02%, REML oscillates 0.00-0.15%)\n266\t    logprob_analysis_feasible: MMLU and ARC only (MC format with logprob access)\n267\t    logprob_item_id_pct_MMLU: 55.3\n268\t    logprob_model_x_item_pct_MMLU: 28.1\n269\t    logprob_residual_pct_MMLU: 11.2\n270\t    logprob_HellaSwag: feasible in principle (completion scoring) but not implemented\n271\t    logprob_GSM8K: not feasible — free-form generation has no natural logprob score\n272\t    model_ranking_stable_across_precision_MMLU: true\n273\t    model_ranking_stable_across_precision_GSM8K: false\n274\t    3way_interactions_total_MMLU_pct: 4.2\n275\t    3way_interactions_total_ARC_pct: 6.8\n276\t    3way_interactions_total_GSM8K_pct: 2.1\n277\t    residual_after_3way_MMLU_pct: 15.3\n278\t    IRT_items_G80_MMLU: 116\n279\t    Gtheory_items_G80_MMLU: 510\n280\t    IRT_to_Gtheory_ratio: 4.4\n281\t    IRT_comparison_feasible_on: MMLU only — other benchmarks lack sufficient data\n282\t      or have pathological structures\n283\t    power_to_detect_5pct_interaction_MMLU: 0.99\n284\t    power_to_detect_5pct_interaction_ARC: 0.94\n285\t    power_to_detect_5pct_interaction_GSM8K: 0.78\n286\t    fixed_vs_random_delta_MMLU_pp: 2.8\n287\t    fixed_vs_random_delta_GSM8K_pp: 8.4\n288\t    bernoulli_p1p_correlation_with_item_var_MMLU: 0.31\n289\t    bernoulli_p1p_correlation_with_item_var_GSM8K: 0.68\n290\t    bootstrap_jackknife_CI_ratio_MMLU: 0.98\n291\t    bootstrap_jackknife_CI_ratio_ARC: 1.03\n292\t    bootstrap_jackknife_CI_ratio_GSM8K: 1.14\n293\t    MMLU_full_vs_matched_2x2_delta_item_id_pp: 2.9\n294\t    MMLU_full_vs_matched_2x2_delta_model_x_item_pp: 1.4\n295\t    G_at_14K_items_MMLU_projection: 0.987\n296\t    G_at_full_ARC_2590_items_projection: 0.953\n297\t    G_at_full_GSM8K_1319_items_projection: 0.812\n298\t    logprob_cross_benchmark: only MMLU and ARC feasible, insufficient for cross-benchmark\n299\t      claims\n300\t    compute_cost_per_G80_MMLU: ~$45 (510 items × 4 models × 6 prompts × 6 seeds)\n301\t    compute_cost_per_G80_GSM8K: ~$210 (1150 items × 4 models × 6 prompts × 6 seeds,\n302\t      longer generation)\n303\t  result_rationale: 'MMLU analyses are strong and largely replicate exp-001 findings\n304\t    with bootstrap CIs and additional robustness checks. The IRT vs G-theory comparison\n305\t    (4.4× item ratio) is clean on MMLU and becomes a primary contribution.\n306\t\n307\t    Cross-benchmark analyses are degraded by three compounding factors: (1) Only 4\n308\t    benchmarks (no MATH) with only 4 models — limited statistical power for cross-benchmark\n309\t    generalizations and wide bootstrap CIs. (2) GSM8K''s pathological variance structure\n310\t    (residual >40%, negative REML estimates) makes several analyses unreliable or\n311\t    uninterpretable on that benchmark. The high residual means G-theory components\n312\t    are estimated with low precision, widening CIs and making D-study projections\n313\t    impractical (CI for G80 items: [600, 2100]). (3) Fixed-vs-random sensitivity analysis\n314\t    fails on GSM8K (Δ>5pp), meaning the methodological choice of treating temperature\n315\t    as random vs fixed materially affects results — another benchmark-specific pathology.\n316\t\n317\t    REML diverges from Henderson I on GSM8K because the balanced design assumption\n318\t    is stressed by near-zero or negative components. Henderson I is unbiased for balanced\n319\t    designs but produces interpretable results even when components are near-zero;\n320\t    REML tries to find ML estimates and can produce negative values that must be clamped.\n321\t    This divergence is itself a finding: Henderson I is reliable for well-behaved\n322\t    MC benchmarks but may be problematic for free-form generation benchmarks.\n323\t\n324\t    The logprob analysis cannot extend beyond MC formats (MMLU, ARC) because free-form\n325\t    and completion tasks lack a natural single-token probability score. This means\n326\t    the \"Evaluation Metrics\" claim in the title is only partially supported, and the\n327\t    title may need adjustment per MD9.\n328\t\n329\t    Bootstrap vs jackknife agreement is good on MMLU/ARC (ratio 0.98-1.03) but marginal\n330\t    on GSM8K (ratio 1.14), suggesting bootstrap CIs on GSM8K are slightly anti-conservative\n331\t    due to non-independence in the stochastic generation process.\n332\t\n333\t    '\n334\t  tables:\n335\t  - name: 'Table: Bootstrap 95% CIs for All Variance Components (MMLU, 4 models)'\n336\t    description: '7 variance components × point estimate + 95% CI. item_id: 42.1%\n337\t      [36.0, 48.8], model×item: 33.6% [28.4, 37.9], residual: 19.5% [16.2, 23.1].\n338\t      All CIs exclude zero for top-4 components. prompt×item and seed×item CIs overlap.\n339\t\n340\t      '\n341\t  - name: 'Table: REML vs Henderson I Comparison Across Benchmarks'\n342\t    description: '4 benchmarks × 7 components × Henderson I estimate + REML estimate\n343\t      + Δ. MMLU: all Δ < 0.5pp. ARC: Δ < 1.2pp. HellaSwag: precision×item oscillates.\n344\t      GSM8K: seed×item diverges (1.8% vs clamped 0%). Conclusion: Henderson I reliable\n345\t      for MC benchmarks; caution needed for free-form generation.\n346\t\n347\t      '\n348\t  - name: 'Table: IRT vs G-Theory Item Requirements (MMLU)'\n349\t    description: 'Reliability threshold × IRT items × G-theory items × ratio. G≥0.70:\n350\t      55 vs 210 (3.8×). G≥0.80: 116 vs 510 (4.4×). G≥0.90: 280 vs 1350 (4.8×). Decomposition\n351\t      of G-theory excess: prompt×item accounts for 22%, seed×item 23%, residual 55%\n352\t      of additional items.\n353\t\n354\t      '\n355\t  - name: 'Table: Fixed vs Random Sensitivity Analysis'\n356\t    description: '4 benchmarks × top-4 components × random estimate + fixed estimate\n357\t      + Δ. MMLU Δ < 3pp (robust). GSM8K Δ = 8.4pp for item_id (NOT robust — temperature\n358\t      reclassification redistributes 8pp from temperature to residual).\n359\t\n360\t      '\n361\t  - name: 'Table: D-Study Budget Comparison with Bootstrap CIs'\n362\t    description: '4 benchmarks × items for G≥0.80 [95% CI]. MMLU: 510 [430, 610].\n363\t      ARC: 340 [260, 470]. HellaSwag: 280 [210, 380]. GSM8K: 1150 [600, 2100]. The\n364\t      GSM8K CI spans 3.5×, making the point estimate practically meaningless.\n365\t\n366\t      '\n367\t  figures_description:\n368\t  - 'Forest plot of variance component CIs across 4 benchmarks. Each benchmark is\n369\t    a panel; within each panel, components are rows with point estimates (dots) and\n370\t    95% CI whiskers. Visually shows that CI widths vary dramatically across benchmarks\n371\t    (tight on MMLU, wide on GSM8K) and component rankings are inconsistent.\n372\t\n373\t    '\n374\t  - 'IRT vs G-theory comparison bar chart (MMLU only). Two bars per reliability threshold\n375\t    (0.70, 0.80, 0.90). G-theory bar decomposed into sub-segments showing which facets\n376\t    (prompt×item, seed×item, residual) account for the extra items beyond IRT. Clear\n377\t    visual showing the \"multi-facet reliability cost.\"\n378\t\n379\t    '\n380\t  - 'D-study projection curves with 95% CI ribbons for 4 benchmarks (overlaid on single\n381\t    panel). MMLU and HellaSwag curves rise steeply with narrow ribbons. GSM8K curve\n382\t    is nearly flat with a wide ribbon that barely touches G=0.80 at n=1150.\n383\t\n384\t    '\n385\t  analysis: 'exp-004 is the analytical core of the paper under worst tier. The MMLU-specific\n386\t    analyses are strong: bootstrap CIs confirm top-3 component ordering, IRT comparison\n387\t    produces the clean 4.4× ratio, REML validates Henderson I, and the binary data\n388\t    robustness checks all pass. These form the backbone of the paper.\n389\t\n390\t    Cross-benchmark analyses reveal the \"irreducibly benchmark-specific\" finding but\n391\t    cannot support strong quantitative claims due to wide CIs and benchmark-specific\n392\t    pathologies. GSM8K is particularly problematic: high residual → wide CIs → unreliable\n393\t    D-study projections → fixed-vs-random sensitivity failure. The paper should present\n394\t    GSM8K results as \"illustrative of pathological variance structure in free-form\n395\t    generation\" rather than as reliable G-theory estimates.\n396\t\n397\t    The IRT vs G-theory comparison is limited to MMLU (the only benchmark with full\n398\t    design + clean variance structure + IRT comparability). This is sufficient for\n399\t    a strong numbered contribution but prevents claiming \"cross-benchmark IRT comparison.\"\n400\t\n401\t    Key paper structure implication: MMLU analysis (exp-001 + exp-004 MMLU-specific)\n402\t    occupies 60-70% of the results section. Cross-benchmark analysis (exp-002 + exp-004\n403\t    cross-benchmark) occupies 30-40% as an \"exploratory extension\" with explicit caveats\n404\t    about reduced design, limited benchmarks, and pathological structures.\n405\t\n406\t    '\n407\t- name: 14B model extension\n408\t  plan_id: exp-005\n409\t  description: 'Blocked. Depends on exp-003 (hardware) which is blocked, and requires\n410\t    ≥48GB VRAM GPUs not currently allocated. No 14B model data is collected.\n411\t\n412\t    '\n413\t  expected_results:\n414\t    status: blocked\n415\t    n_14B_models_tested: 0\n416\t    scale_analysis_completed: false\n417\t    reason: Depends on exp-003 (blocked); requires high-VRAM GPU not available\n418\t  result_rationale: 'exp-005 has two blocking dependencies: (1) it was designed to\n419\t    run on datacenter GPUs at BF16/FP16 after exp-003 establishes the multi-hardware\n420\t    baseline, and (2) 14B models require ≥48GB VRAM for comfortable inference with\n421\t    vLLM, which is available on the existing RTX PRO 6000 (96GB) but the GPU budget\n422\t    is fully consumed by exp-002 Phase 1. Even if the hardware dependency were relaxed,\n423\t    the compute hours (100h) compete directly with exp-002''s needs. Under worst-tier\n424\t    resource constraints, P1 experiments are systematically deprioritized in favor\n425\t    of P0 experiments.\n426\t\n427\t    Impact on paper: The \"model scale\" dimension is entirely absent. The paper must\n428\t    state \"all models are 7-9B parameter scale\" as a limitation. This is defensible\n429\t    — the paper studies variance structure, not scaling laws — but reviewers may flag\n430\t    it. The planned comparison between dense (Qwen3-14B) and reasoning-distilled (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B)\n431\t    architectures is lost. This comparison would have been interesting for the model×item\n432\t    interaction analysis (does distillation change the interaction pattern?) but is\n433\t    not essential for the core claims.\n434\t\n435\t    '\n436\t  tables: []\n437\t  figures_description: []\n438\t  analysis: 'No analysis possible. Limitation statement: \"Our analysis is limited\n439\t    to 7-9B parameter open-weight models. Whether variance structure changes with\n440\t    model scale is an important open question — larger models may show reduced model×item\n441\t    interaction if they converge toward similar capability profiles, or increased\n442\t    interaction if scale enables more specialized item-level behaviors.\"\n443\t\n444\t    '\n445\t- name: Fine-tuning G-study\n446\t  plan_id: exp-006\n447\t  description: 'Blocked by compute reallocation. The 1,728 fine-tuning runs (432 per\n448\t    task × 4 tasks) require ~200 GPU hours on westd-32570, which is fully allocated\n449\t    to exp-002 Phase 1 and exp-001 post-processing. Even partial completion (1 task)\n450\t    would consume 50+ hours.\n451\t\n452\t    '\n453\t  expected_results:\n454\t    status: blocked\n455\t    n_finetuning_runs_completed: 0\n456\t    tasks_completed: 0\n457\t    reason: GPU budget fully consumed by P0 experiments (exp-002 Phase 1)\n458\t  result_rationale: 'exp-006 is independent (no experimental dependencies) but competes\n459\t    for the same GPU resource. Under worst-tier constraints, exp-002 Phase 1 alone\n460\t    consumes 200-250 GPU hours (4 models × 4 benchmarks × ~15h per model-benchmark\n461\t    combination with the full prompt/seed/ordering crossing). With exp-001 post-processing\n462\t    reruns and validation checks, the total GPU budget is exhausted before exp-006\n463\t    can start.\n464\t\n465\t    Even if exp-006 were partially completed (e.g., SST-2 only), the results would\n466\t    be insufficient for even a Discussion-section preliminary observation — a single\n467\t    fine-tuning task cannot support variance structure comparison between inference\n468\t    and fine-tuning paradigms.\n469\t\n470\t    Impact on paper: Fine-tuning analysis is mentioned as future work in Discussion.\n471\t    The paper explicitly qualifies: \"Our analysis addresses inference-time evaluation\n472\t    only. Fine-tuning variance structure — where learning rate, data order, and seed\n473\t    interact with task-specific loss landscapes — is a qualitatively different problem\n474\t    that we leave for future work.\" Per D006 Dream #3 directive, fine-tuning was already\n475\t    downgraded to Discussion-section preliminary observation, so blockage has minimal\n476\t    impact on paper structure.\n477\t\n478\t    '\n479\t  tables: []\n480\t  figures_description: []\n481\t  analysis: 'No analysis possible. The fine-tuning extension is entirely absent from\n482\t    the paper. Per existing framing directives, this was already downgraded to Discussion\n483\t    section. Cite Bui et al. (2025) for evidence that fine-tuning variance structure\n484\t    differs substantially (RTES variance=18.22 for RoBERTa on COPA) and position as\n485\t    motivated future work.\n486\t\n487\t    '\n488\t- name: LayerCast precision study\n489\t  plan_id: exp-007\n490\t  description: 'Deprioritized (P2). Not started due to GPU budget exhaustion from\n491\t    P0 experiments. LayerCast (FP16 storage + FP32 compute) as a variance mitigation\n492\t    strategy is not tested.\n493\t\n494\t    '\n495\t  expected_results:\n496\t    status: blocked\n497\t    layercast_tested: false\n498\t    variance_reduction_quantified: false\n499\t    reason: P2 priority; GPU budget exhausted by P0 experiments\n500\t  result_rationale: 'exp-007 is the lowest-priority experiment (P2) and depends on\n501\t    exp-001 being complete (it is). However, the 30 GPU hours required compete with\n502\t    exp-002 Phase 1 needs. Under worst-tier constraints, all discretionary GPU budget\n503\t    is consumed by the P0 pipeline. Additionally, the scientific value of LayerCast\n504\t    testing is reduced once BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity analysis (already completed in\n505\t    exp-001) shows BF16≈FP16 (Δ=0.23pp) — if standard precision choices don''t matter,\n506\t    testing a mixed-precision strategy is low priority.\n507\t\n508\t    Impact on paper: No variance mitigation strategy is tested. The paper''s D-study\n509\t    budgets address \"how many items/prompts/seeds\" but not \"how to reduce precision\n510\t    variance.\" This is consistent with the finding that precision contributes <0.1%\n511\t    variance under binary scoring — there is nothing to mitigate. The paper can note:\n512\t    \"LayerCast and other mixed-precision strategies may be relevant for text-exact-match\n513\t    scoring where precision×temperature interaction is detectable (1.3%).\"\n514\t\n515\t    '\n516\t  tables: []\n517\t  figures_description: []\n518\t  analysis: 'No analysis possible. Minimal impact on paper — precision is already\n519\t    shown to be negligible under binary scoring. LayerCast is mentioned as a potential\n520\t    mitigation for text-exact-match scoring in the Discussion section.\n521\t\n522\t    '\n523\t- name: MATH benchmark (merged into exp-002 Phase 2)\n524\t  plan_id: exp-008\n525\t  description: 'Blocked. MATH was merged into exp-002 as the 5th benchmark in Phase\n526\t    2, which is blocked by answer extraction pipeline issues. Free-form symbolic math\n527\t    answer normalization (handling equivalent forms: \"1/2\", \"0.5\", \"\\\\frac{1}{2}\",\n528\t    \"0.500\") requires engineering work that stalls.\n529\t\n530\t    '\n531\t  expected_results:\n532\t    status: blocked\n533\t    math_items_collected: 0\n534\t    answer_extraction_pipeline_status: incomplete — symbolic normalization handles\n535\t      ~70% of MATH answers correctly, remaining 30% require regex/sympy extensions\n536\t    reason: Merged into exp-002 Phase 2; blocked by answer normalization engineering\n537\t  result_rationale: 'MATH benchmark presents unique data engineering challenges compared\n538\t    to MC benchmarks (MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag) and even compared to GSM8K (which has\n539\t    simpler numerical answers). MATH answers include symbolic expressions, fractions,\n540\t    roots, and multiple equivalent forms that require robust normalization. The extraction\n541\t    pipeline handles simple numerical answers (GSM8K-style) but fails on ~30% of MATH\n542\t    items where the answer is symbolic. Building a robust sympy-based normalization\n543\t    pipeline is estimated at 20-30 engineering hours — work that is deprioritized\n544\t    under worst-tier constraints where the focus is on completing Phase 1 analysis\n545\t    and writing.\n546\t\n547\t    Impact on paper: The absence of MATH critically undermines the within-format gradient\n548\t    test. With only GSM8K as a free-form benchmark, the within-format gradient analysis\n549\t    has no free-form comparator. The paper cannot distinguish whether GSM8K''s low\n550\t    item_id% (35.4%) is due to free-form format, mathematical reasoning task type,\n551\t    or stochastic generation. This ambiguity is a significant limitation for the \"benchmark-specific\n552\t    fingerprints\" framing — without MATH, the paper cannot determine whether the fingerprint\n553\t    is driven by format or task content within the free-form category.\n554\t\n555\t    The paper addresses this by: (a) acknowledging the limitation explicitly, (b)\n556\t    noting that MATH''s absence prevents within-free-form gradient testing, and (c)\n557\t    framing MATH as the highest-priority extension for future work. The \"benchmark-specific\n558\t    fingerprints\" claim is still supported by the MC benchmarks (MMLU vs ARC have\n559\t    different structures despite same format) and the cross-format comparison (MC\n560\t    vs completion vs free-form), but the within-format argument is weaker.\n561\t\n562\t    '\n563\t  tables: []\n564\t  figures_description: []\n565\t  analysis: No analysis possible. The paper's cross-benchmark analysis is limited\n566\t    to 4 benchmarks (MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag, GSM8K) spanning 3 formats (MC, completion,\n567\t    free-form) but with only 1-2 benchmarks per format. Within-format gradient testing\n568\t    is limited to MC (MMLU vs ARC = 2 data points). The \"benchmark-specific fingerprints\"\n569\t    finding survives but is less compelling without a within-format comparator for\n570\t    free-form tasks.\n571\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b38b24c5-912c-4a28-9ffb-691a58265a42","uuid":"69033186-ed3e-4050-9bac-c6d9f78d1f8a","timestamp":"2026-05-21T13:49:14.351Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T122449/worst/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Abandon cross-benchmark gradient as headline finding. The paper\n  pivots to two axes: (A) deep single-benchmark MMLU analysis (model×item quantification,\n  D-study out-of-sample validation, IRT comparison, full robustness package — all\n  already strong from exp-001/exp-004), and (B) ''benchmark-specific variance fingerprints''\n  — each benchmark produces an idiosyncratic variance structure that cannot be predicted\n  from task taxonomy, format, or difficulty distribution alone. The hook becomes:\n  \"not only is there no universal reproducibility recipe — there is no universal variance\n  structure, and even task-type taxonomy fails to predict it.\" This is arguably more\n  surprising than a clean gradient. Claim 1 narrowed to MMLU with exploratory multi-benchmark\n  extension. Claim 3 strengthened — benchmark-specific budgets are the ONLY viable\n  approach because variance fingerprints are irreducibly benchmark-specific. Primary\n  contribution shifts from gradient discovery to: (i) model×item interaction quantification\n  with D-study validation on MMLU, (ii) IRT vs G-theory comparison, (iii) demonstration\n  that variance structure is irreducibly benchmark-specific. Same AAAI target but\n  likely needs ACL ARR October cycle for rewriting.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: Multi-benchmark replication (Phase 1 only)\n  plan_id: exp-002\n  description: 'Phase 1 completes: 4 original models (Llama-3.1-8B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B,\n    Qwen3-8B) × 4 benchmarks (MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, GSM8K) × BF16 × 2 temps\n    (0.0, 0.7) × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 4 orderings × 200 items each. Phase 2 (MATH)\n    blocked — dataset preprocessing issues with free-form answer extraction pipeline;\n    answer normalization for symbolic math expressions requires engineering work beyond\n    available bandwidth. Phase 3 (Yi, OLMo) blocked — westd-15695 unavailable due\n    to cluster maintenance or allocation conflict. Phase 4 (InternLM, DeepSeek) blocked\n    — depends on Phase 3 infrastructure.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_benchmarks_completed: 4\n    n_models_completed: 4\n    total_records_per_benchmark: 115200\n    total_records_all: 460800\n    item_id_pct_MMLU: 41.8\n    item_id_pct_ARC: 37.2\n    item_id_pct_HellaSwag: 44.1\n    item_id_pct_GSM8K: 35.4\n    gradient_spread_item_id_pp: 8.7\n    gradient_ordering: HellaSwag > MMLU > ARC > GSM8K\n    gradient_monotonicity_by_task_complexity: false\n    gradient_permutation_test_p: 0.41\n    model_x_item_pct_MMLU: 33.8\n    model_x_item_pct_ARC: 18.3\n    model_x_item_pct_HellaSwag: 15.7\n    model_x_item_pct_GSM8K: 38.9\n    model_x_item_range_pp: 23.2\n    residual_pct_MMLU: 19.5\n    residual_pct_ARC: 28.4\n    residual_pct_HellaSwag: 25.3\n    residual_pct_GSM8K: 43.2\n    G_item_MMLU: 0.821\n    G_item_ARC: 0.714\n    G_item_HellaSwag: 0.783\n    G_item_GSM8K: 0.518\n    G_item_ordering: MMLU > HellaSwag > ARC > GSM8K\n    D_study_items_G80_MMLU: 510\n    D_study_items_G80_ARC: 340\n    D_study_items_G80_HellaSwag: 280\n    D_study_items_G80_GSM8K: 1150\n    D_study_range: 280-1150 (4.1× spread)\n    Henderson_I_convergence_issues: GSM8K seed×item negative REML estimate (-0.3%),\n      clamped to zero; HellaSwag precision×item near-zero (0.02%), REML estimate oscillates\n    within_MC_gradient_MMLU_vs_ARC: 42% vs 37% = 5pp, but ARC < MMLU contradicts complexity\n      prediction (ARC is harder reasoning)\n    within_format_gradient_preserved: false\n    accuracy_MMLU: 0.681\n    accuracy_ARC: 0.592\n    accuracy_HellaSwag: 0.743\n    accuracy_GSM8K: 0.483\n    prompt_x_item_pct_MMLU: 4.2\n    prompt_x_item_pct_ARC: 6.8\n    prompt_x_item_pct_HellaSwag: 3.1\n    prompt_x_item_pct_GSM8K: 2.4\n  result_rationale: 'Phase 1 completion with Phase 2-4 blockage: MATH requires robust\n    free-form answer extraction (symbolic expressions, equivalent forms like \"1/2\"\n    vs \"0.5\" vs \"\\\\frac{1}{2}\"), which stalls on engineering work. Additional models\n    blocked by hardware unavailability.\n\n    No systematic gradient: The variance structure is driven by benchmark-specific\n    properties that don''t align with any single task taxonomy axis. HellaSwag shows\n    highest item_id% (44.1%) despite being \"commonsense completion\" — not the most\n    complex task — because its items have extremely stable difficulty rankings across\n    models (4 candidates with clear plausibility ordering). GSM8K shows lowest item_id%\n    (35.4%) and highest residual (43.2%) because free-form math generation introduces\n    stochastic variation from decoding that inflates the residual; the same model\n    solving the same problem with the same prompt/seed can produce different solution\n    paths and occasionally different final answers at T>0.\n\n    Model×item interaction is inconsistent: GSM8K has the highest model×item (38.9%)\n    because models have strongly differentiated math capabilities — Qwen3-8B solves\n    multi-step problems that Mistral-7B cannot, and vice versa for word problems with\n    unusual phrasing. HellaSwag has lowest model×item (15.7%) because commonsense\n    completion difficulty is more universal across model families. This pattern (math\n    > commonsense for model×item) is interpretable post-hoc but was not predictable\n    from task taxonomy alone.\n\n    ARC convergence: Henderson I produces near-zero estimates for several 3-way interactions\n    because the reduced design (2 temps × bf16-only) has limited degrees of freedom\n    for higher-order terms. REML on GSM8K produces a negative variance component for\n    seed×item, which is clamped to zero — seed choice has negligible systematic effect\n    on item difficulty in free-form generation because the dominant stochasticity\n    is in the generation process itself, not in CUDA-level seed effects.\n\n    D-study budgets span 4.1× (280-1150 items for G≥0.80), but this range has no interpretable\n    pattern: HellaSwag needs fewest items (high item_id%, low residual → efficient\n    measurement) while GSM8K needs most (low item_id%, high residual → measurement\n    is noisy). This is not a \"gradient\" — it''s a benchmark-specific fingerprint determined\n    by format, generation stochasticity, and model-task interaction structure.\n\n    Within-format gradient fails: Within MC format, MMLU (42%) > ARC (37%), but ARC\n    contains harder science reasoning items — if a task-complexity gradient existed,\n    ARC should be lower OR higher than MMLU depending on the hypothesized direction.\n    The 5pp difference is more likely explained by item pool characteristics (ARC\n    has fewer items with extreme difficulty, compressing item_id%) than by task complexity.\n    With only two MC benchmarks and no within- format gradient, the format confound\n    cannot be resolved or ruled out.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Cross-Benchmark Variance Decomposition (Phase 1, 4 models × 4 benchmarks)'\n    description: 'Rows: variance components (item_id, model, model×item, prompt×item,\n      seed×item, temperature, temperature×item, residual). Columns: MMLU, ARC-Challenge,\n      HellaSwag, GSM8K. Shows no systematic gradient — component rankings differ across\n      benchmarks. GSM8K residual dominates (43.2%); HellaSwag item_id highest (44.1%);\n      model×item ranges from 15.7% (HellaSwag) to 38.9% (GSM8K) without pattern.\n\n      '\n  - name: 'Table: D-Study Budget Comparison Across Benchmarks'\n    description: 'Items required for G≥0.70/0.80/0.90 per benchmark. Shows 4.1× spread\n      in G≥0.80 requirements (280-1150 items). No interpretable pattern linking budget\n      to task type.\n\n      '\n  - name: 'Table: Within-Format Gradient Test'\n    description: 'MC format: MMLU (42%) vs ARC (37%), Δ=5pp. Free-form: GSM8K only\n      (35.4%), no comparator (MATH blocked). Completion: HellaSwag only (44.1%). Conclusion:\n      within-format gradient NOT preserved; insufficient data to disentangle format\n      from task-type effects.\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Stacked-bar variance decomposition across 4 benchmarks — the \"anti-gradient\"\n    figure. No systematic left-to-right ordering; bars show qualitatively different\n    component structures. GSM8K bar dominated by residual (gray, 43%); HellaSwag dominated\n    by item_id (blue, 44%); model×item (orange) varies 3× across benchmarks. Horizontal\n    dashed line at item_id=42% (MMLU reference) shows other benchmarks scatter above\n    and below without trend.\n\n    '\n  - 'D-study projection curves per benchmark (G vs item count, 4 panels or 4 lines\n    on one panel). Curves have wildly different slopes and asymptotes. GSM8K curve\n    is nearly flat — even 1000 items only reaches G≈0.75 because residual dominates.\n    HellaSwag curve rises steeply, crossing G=0.80 at ~280 items.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'The cross-benchmark gradient — the planned headline finding — does not\n    materialize. Item_id% varies only 8.7pp across benchmarks (35-44%), with no monotonic\n    ordering by task complexity, format, or any other interpretable dimension. The\n    permutation test for monotonicity yields p=0.41 (null). Model×item interaction\n    varies 23pp across benchmarks but in an unpredictable pattern (highest on GSM8K,\n    lowest on HellaSwag).\n\n    However, the \"benchmark-specific variance fingerprints\" finding is itself publishable.\n    The fact that variance structure is irreducibly benchmark-specific — not predictable\n    from task taxonomy — means (a) benchmark-specific G-studies are mandatory, not\n    optional, (b) reproducibility budgets cannot be transferred across benchmarks,\n    and (c) conclusions about \"which factors matter\" from single-benchmark studies\n    do not generalize. This is arguably more surprising and practically important\n    than a clean gradient.\n\n    GSM8K''s pathological residual (43.2%) is a standalone finding: free-form generation\n    introduces irreducible stochasticity that makes G-theory decomposition qualitatively\n    different from MC benchmarks. The seed×item negative REML estimate and convergence\n    oscillation on HellaSwag precision×item suggest that Henderson I (designed for\n    balanced continuous data) struggles with certain benchmark-specific variance structures,\n    strengthening the case for benchmark-specific methodology.\n\n    Phase 2-4 blockage limits the analysis: no MATH benchmark prevents within-free-form\n    gradient testing, and only 4 models prevents the planned expansion to 6-8 models\n    that would strengthen the model-as-facet exchangeability argument. The paper must\n    explicitly scope to \"4 models × 4 benchmarks, exploratory Phase 1 analysis.\"\n\n    '\n- name: Multi-hardware G-study\n  plan_id: exp-003\n  description: 'Completely blocked. No second GPU type becomes available during the\n    experimental window. Hardware claim remains scoped to \"numerical precision on\n    RTX PRO 6000 (Blackwell).\"\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    status: blocked\n    n_gpu_types: 1\n    data_collected: 0\n    hardware_crossing_completed: false\n    reason: No alternative GPU allocation (A100/H100/AMD) secured\n  result_rationale: 'The experiment requires a fundamentally different GPU architecture\n    to test hardware×methodology interaction. In the worst tier, the compute allocation\n    is fully consumed by exp-002 Phase 1 on the single available machine (westd-32570).\n    The planned second machine (westd-15695) is either unavailable, has the same GPU\n    type, or has allocation conflicts. Without a second GPU type, the entire experiment\n    is infeasible.\n\n    Impact on paper: Claim 2 must be scoped to \"precision separability on a single\n    GPU architecture\" rather than \"infrastructure separability.\" This is already the\n    planned defensive scoping per D003/D007. The paper acknowledges hardware crossing\n    as future work and cites Yuan et al. (2025) for evidence that hardware effects\n    exist but are unquantified in a G-theory framework. This weakens but does not\n    kill Claim 2 — precision separability under binary scoring is still a valid confirmatory\n    finding.\n\n    '\n  tables: []\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: 'No analysis possible. Experiment contributes zero data. Paper must explicitly\n    state \"hardware limited to a single GPU architecture (NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000, Blackwell)\"\n    in Methods and Limitations. The separability claim (Claim 2) survives but is narrowly\n    scoped — \"precision × methodology separability\" rather than \"infrastructure ×\n    methodology separability.\" Reviewer concern #1 (\"Only N GPU types\") is unaddressed\n    and must be handled as a stated limitation with future work framing.\n\n    '\n- name: Comprehensive analysis\n  plan_id: exp-004\n  description: 'Analysis proceeds on available data: exp-001 MMLU (345,600 records,\n    4 models, full design) + exp-002 Phase 1 (460,800 records, 4 models × 4 benchmarks,\n    reduced design). Most sub-analyses (a)-(q) are feasible on MMLU. Cross-benchmark\n    sub-analyses are limited by exp-002''s reduced scope and pathological variance\n    structures.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    model_as_facet_MMLU_item_id_pct: 42.1\n    model_as_facet_MMLU_model_x_item_pct: 33.6\n    model_as_facet_other_benchmarks: attempted but unreliable on GSM8K (residual >40%\n      absorbs signal)\n    bootstrap_n_resamples: 1000\n    bootstrap_CI_item_id_MMLU:\n    - 36.0\n    - 48.8\n    bootstrap_CI_model_x_item_MMLU:\n    - 28.4\n    - 37.9\n    bootstrap_CI_width_item_id_MMLU_pp: 12.8\n    bootstrap_CI_cross_benchmark: wide — ARC item_id CI [28, 47], GSM8K item_id CI\n      [24, 48]\n    bootstrap_CI_GSM8K_residual:\n    - 35.1\n    - 52.8\n    D_study_MMLU_items_G80_CI:\n    - 430\n    - 610\n    D_study_MMLU_items_G90_CI:\n    - 1050\n    - 1680\n    D_study_cross_benchmark_CIs: 'too wide for practical guidance on GSM8K (G80: [600,\n      2100])'\n    REML_Henderson_agreement_MMLU: all components within <0.5pp\n    REML_Henderson_agreement_ARC: within <1.2pp for top-3 components\n    REML_Henderson_agreement_GSM8K: diverge — Henderson I seed×item=1.8%, REML=-0.3%\n      (clamped to 0)\n    REML_Henderson_agreement_HellaSwag: within <0.8pp except precision×item (Henderson\n      0.02%, REML oscillates 0.00-0.15%)\n    logprob_analysis_feasible: MMLU and ARC only (MC format with logprob access)\n    logprob_item_id_pct_MMLU: 55.3\n    logprob_model_x_item_pct_MMLU: 28.1\n    logprob_residual_pct_MMLU: 11.2\n    logprob_HellaSwag: feasible in principle (completion scoring) but not implemented\n    logprob_GSM8K: not feasible — free-form generation has no natural logprob score\n    model_ranking_stable_across_precision_MMLU: true\n    model_ranking_stable_across_precision_GSM8K: false\n    3way_interactions_total_MMLU_pct: 4.2\n    3way_interactions_total_ARC_pct: 6.8\n    3way_interactions_total_GSM8K_pct: 2.1\n    residual_after_3way_MMLU_pct: 15.3\n    IRT_items_G80_MMLU: 116\n    Gtheory_items_G80_MMLU: 510\n    IRT_to_Gtheory_ratio: 4.4\n    IRT_comparison_feasible_on: MMLU only — other benchmarks lack sufficient data\n      or have pathological structures\n    power_to_detect_5pct_interaction_MMLU: 0.99\n    power_to_detect_5pct_interaction_ARC: 0.94\n    power_to_detect_5pct_interaction_GSM8K: 0.78\n    fixed_vs_random_delta_MMLU_pp: 2.8\n    fixed_vs_random_delta_GSM8K_pp: 8.4\n    bernoulli_p1p_correlation_with_item_var_MMLU: 0.31\n    bernoulli_p1p_correlation_with_item_var_GSM8K: 0.68\n    bootstrap_jackknife_CI_ratio_MMLU: 0.98\n    bootstrap_jackknife_CI_ratio_ARC: 1.03\n    bootstrap_jackknife_CI_ratio_GSM8K: 1.14\n    MMLU_full_vs_matched_2x2_delta_item_id_pp: 2.9\n    MMLU_full_vs_matched_2x2_delta_model_x_item_pp: 1.4\n    G_at_14K_items_MMLU_projection: 0.987\n    G_at_full_ARC_2590_items_projection: 0.953\n    G_at_full_GSM8K_1319_items_projection: 0.812\n    logprob_cross_benchmark: only MMLU and ARC feasible, insufficient for cross-benchmark\n      claims\n    compute_cost_per_G80_MMLU: ~$45 (510 items × 4 models × 6 prompts × 6 seeds)\n    compute_cost_per_G80_GSM8K: ~$210 (1150 items × 4 models × 6 prompts × 6 seeds,\n      longer generation)\n  result_rationale: 'MMLU analyses are strong and largely replicate exp-001 findings\n    with bootstrap CIs and additional robustness checks. The IRT vs G-theory comparison\n    (4.4× item ratio) is clean on MMLU and becomes a primary contribution.\n\n    Cross-benchmark analyses are degraded by three compounding factors: (1) Only 4\n    benchmarks (no MATH) with only 4 models — limited statistical power for cross-benchmark\n    generalizations and wide bootstrap CIs. (2) GSM8K''s pathological variance structure\n    (residual >40%, negative REML estimates) makes several analyses unreliable or\n    uninterpretable on that benchmark. The high residual means G-theory components\n    are estimated with low precision, widening CIs and making D-study projections\n    impractical (CI for G80 items: [600, 2100]). (3) Fixed-vs-random sensitivity analysis\n    fails on GSM8K (Δ>5pp), meaning the methodological choice of treating temperature\n    as random vs fixed materially affects results — another benchmark-specific pathology.\n\n    REML diverges from Henderson I on GSM8K because the balanced design assumption\n    is stressed by near-zero or negative components. Henderson I is unbiased for balanced\n    designs but produces interpretable results even when components are near-zero;\n    REML tries to find ML estimates and can produce negative values that must be clamped.\n    This divergence is itself a finding: Henderson I is reliable for well-behaved\n    MC benchmarks but may be problematic for free-form generation benchmarks.\n\n    The logprob analysis cannot extend beyond MC formats (MMLU, ARC) because free-form\n    and completion tasks lack a natural single-token probability score. This means\n    the \"Evaluation Metrics\" claim in the title is only partially supported, and the\n    title may need adjustment per MD9.\n\n    Bootstrap vs jackknife agreement is good on MMLU/ARC (ratio 0.98-1.03) but marginal\n    on GSM8K (ratio 1.14), suggesting bootstrap CIs on GSM8K are slightly anti-conservative\n    due to non-independence in the stochastic generation process.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Bootstrap 95% CIs for All Variance Components (MMLU, 4 models)'\n    description: '7 variance components × point estimate + 95% CI. item_id: 42.1%\n      [36.0, 48.8], model×item: 33.6% [28.4, 37.9], residual: 19.5% [16.2, 23.1].\n      All CIs exclude zero for top-4 components. prompt×item and seed×item CIs overlap.\n\n      '\n  - name: 'Table: REML vs Henderson I Comparison Across Benchmarks'\n    description: '4 benchmarks × 7 components × Henderson I estimate + REML estimate\n      + Δ. MMLU: all Δ < 0.5pp. ARC: Δ < 1.2pp. HellaSwag: precision×item oscillates.\n      GSM8K: seed×item diverges (1.8% vs clamped 0%). Conclusion: Henderson I reliable\n      for MC benchmarks; caution needed for free-form generation.\n\n      '\n  - name: 'Table: IRT vs G-Theory Item Requirements (MMLU)'\n    description: 'Reliability threshold × IRT items × G-theory items × ratio. G≥0.70:\n      55 vs 210 (3.8×). G≥0.80: 116 vs 510 (4.4×). G≥0.90: 280 vs 1350 (4.8×). Decomposition\n      of G-theory excess: prompt×item accounts for 22%, seed×item 23%, residual 55%\n      of additional items.\n\n      '\n  - name: 'Table: Fixed vs Random Sensitivity Analysis'\n    description: '4 benchmarks × top-4 components × random estimate + fixed estimate\n      + Δ. MMLU Δ < 3pp (robust). GSM8K Δ = 8.4pp for item_id (NOT robust — temperature\n      reclassification redistributes 8pp from temperature to residual).\n\n      '\n  - name: 'Table: D-Study Budget Comparison with Bootstrap CIs'\n    description: '4 benchmarks × items for G≥0.80 [95% CI]. MMLU: 510 [430, 610].\n      ARC: 340 [260, 470]. HellaSwag: 280 [210, 380]. GSM8K: 1150 [600, 2100]. The\n      GSM8K CI spans 3.5×, making the point estimate practically meaningless.\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Forest plot of variance component CIs across 4 benchmarks. Each benchmark is\n    a panel; within each panel, components are rows with point estimates (dots) and\n    95% CI whiskers. Visually shows that CI widths vary dramatically across benchmarks\n    (tight on MMLU, wide on GSM8K) and component rankings are inconsistent.\n\n    '\n  - 'IRT vs G-theory comparison bar chart (MMLU only). Two bars per reliability threshold\n    (0.70, 0.80, 0.90). G-theory bar decomposed into sub-segments showing which facets\n    (prompt×item, seed×item, residual) account for the extra items beyond IRT. Clear\n    visual showing the \"multi-facet reliability cost.\"\n\n    '\n  - 'D-study projection curves with 95% CI ribbons for 4 benchmarks (overlaid on single\n    panel). MMLU and HellaSwag curves rise steeply with narrow ribbons. GSM8K curve\n    is nearly flat with a wide ribbon that barely touches G=0.80 at n=1150.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'exp-004 is the analytical core of the paper under worst tier. The MMLU-specific\n    analyses are strong: bootstrap CIs confirm top-3 component ordering, IRT comparison\n    produces the clean 4.4× ratio, REML validates Henderson I, and the binary data\n    robustness checks all pass. These form the backbone of the paper.\n\n    Cross-benchmark analyses reveal the \"irreducibly benchmark-specific\" finding but\n    cannot support strong quantitative claims due to wide CIs and benchmark-specific\n    pathologies. GSM8K is particularly problematic: high residual → wide CIs → unreliable\n    D-study projections → fixed-vs-random sensitivity failure. The paper should present\n    GSM8K results as \"illustrative of pathological variance structure in free-form\n    generation\" rather than as reliable G-theory estimates.\n\n    The IRT vs G-theory comparison is limited to MMLU (the only benchmark with full\n    design + clean variance structure + IRT comparability). This is sufficient for\n    a strong numbered contribution but prevents claiming \"cross-benchmark IRT comparison.\"\n\n    Key paper structure implication: MMLU analysis (exp-001 + exp-004 MMLU-specific)\n    occupies 60-70% of the results section. Cross-benchmark analysis (exp-002 + exp-004\n    cross-benchmark) occupies 30-40% as an \"exploratory extension\" with explicit caveats\n    about reduced design, limited benchmarks, and pathological structures.\n\n    '\n- name: 14B model extension\n  plan_id: exp-005\n  description: 'Blocked. Depends on exp-003 (hardware) which is blocked, and requires\n    ≥48GB VRAM GPUs not currently allocated. No 14B model data is collected.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    status: blocked\n    n_14B_models_tested: 0\n    scale_analysis_completed: false\n    reason: Depends on exp-003 (blocked); requires high-VRAM GPU not available\n  result_rationale: 'exp-005 has two blocking dependencies: (1) it was designed to\n    run on datacenter GPUs at BF16/FP16 after exp-003 establishes the multi-hardware\n    baseline, and (2) 14B models require ≥48GB VRAM for comfortable inference with\n    vLLM, which is available on the existing RTX PRO 6000 (96GB) but the GPU budget\n    is fully consumed by exp-002 Phase 1. Even if the hardware dependency were relaxed,\n    the compute hours (100h) compete directly with exp-002''s needs. Under worst-tier\n    resource constraints, P1 experiments are systematically deprioritized in favor\n    of P0 experiments.\n\n    Impact on paper: The \"model scale\" dimension is entirely absent. The paper must\n    state \"all models are 7-9B parameter scale\" as a limitation. This is defensible\n    — the paper studies variance structure, not scaling laws — but reviewers may flag\n    it. The planned comparison between dense (Qwen3-14B) and reasoning-distilled (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B)\n    architectures is lost. This comparison would have been interesting for the model×item\n    interaction analysis (does distillation change the interaction pattern?) but is\n    not essential for the core claims.\n\n    '\n  tables: []\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: 'No analysis possible. Limitation statement: \"Our analysis is limited\n    to 7-9B parameter open-weight models. Whether variance structure changes with\n    model scale is an important open question — larger models may show reduced model×item\n    interaction if they converge toward similar capability profiles, or increased\n    interaction if scale enables more specialized item-level behaviors.\"\n\n    '\n- name: Fine-tuning G-study\n  plan_id: exp-006\n  description: 'Blocked by compute reallocation. The 1,728 fine-tuning runs (432 per\n    task × 4 tasks) require ~200 GPU hours on westd-32570, which is fully allocated\n    to exp-002 Phase 1 and exp-001 post-processing. Even partial completion (1 task)\n    would consume 50+ hours.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    status: blocked\n    n_finetuning_runs_completed: 0\n    tasks_completed: 0\n    reason: GPU budget fully consumed by P0 experiments (exp-002 Phase 1)\n  result_rationale: 'exp-006 is independent (no experimental dependencies) but competes\n    for the same GPU resource. Under worst-tier constraints, exp-002 Phase 1 alone\n    consumes 200-250 GPU hours (4 models × 4 benchmarks × ~15h per model-benchmark\n    combination with the full prompt/seed/ordering crossing). With exp-001 post-processing\n    reruns and validation checks, the total GPU budget is exhausted before exp-006\n    can start.\n\n    Even if exp-006 were partially completed (e.g., SST-2 only), the results would\n    be insufficient for even a Discussion-section preliminary observation — a single\n    fine-tuning task cannot support variance structure comparison between inference\n    and fine-tuning paradigms.\n\n    Impact on paper: Fine-tuning analysis is mentioned as future work in Discussion.\n    The paper explicitly qualifies: \"Our analysis addresses inference-time evaluation\n    only. Fine-tuning variance structure — where learning rate, data order, and seed\n    interact with task-specific loss landscapes — is a qualitatively different problem\n    that we leave for future work.\" Per D006 Dream #3 directive, fine-tuning was already\n    downgraded to Discussion-section preliminary observation, so blockage has minimal\n    impact on paper structure.\n\n    '\n  tables: []\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: 'No analysis possible. The fine-tuning extension is entirely absent from\n    the paper. Per existing framing directives, this was already downgraded to Discussion\n    section. Cite Bui et al. (2025) for evidence that fine-tuning variance structure\n    differs substantially (RTES variance=18.22 for RoBERTa on COPA) and position as\n    motivated future work.\n\n    '\n- name: LayerCast precision study\n  plan_id: exp-007\n  description: 'Deprioritized (P2). Not started due to GPU budget exhaustion from\n    P0 experiments. LayerCast (FP16 storage + FP32 compute) as a variance mitigation\n    strategy is not tested.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    status: blocked\n    layercast_tested: false\n    variance_reduction_quantified: false\n    reason: P2 priority; GPU budget exhausted by P0 experiments\n  result_rationale: 'exp-007 is the lowest-priority experiment (P2) and depends on\n    exp-001 being complete (it is). However, the 30 GPU hours required compete with\n    exp-002 Phase 1 needs. Under worst-tier constraints, all discretionary GPU budget\n    is consumed by the P0 pipeline. Additionally, the scientific value of LayerCast\n    testing is reduced once BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity analysis (already completed in\n    exp-001) shows BF16≈FP16 (Δ=0.23pp) — if standard precision choices don''t matter,\n    testing a mixed-precision strategy is low priority.\n\n    Impact on paper: No variance mitigation strategy is tested. The paper''s D-study\n    budgets address \"how many items/prompts/seeds\" but not \"how to reduce precision\n    variance.\" This is consistent with the finding that precision contributes <0.1%\n    variance under binary scoring — there is nothing to mitigate. The paper can note:\n    \"LayerCast and other mixed-precision strategies may be relevant for text-exact-match\n    scoring where precision×temperature interaction is detectable (1.3%).\"\n\n    '\n  tables: []\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: 'No analysis possible. Minimal impact on paper — precision is already\n    shown to be negligible under binary scoring. LayerCast is mentioned as a potential\n    mitigation for text-exact-match scoring in the Discussion section.\n\n    '\n- name: MATH benchmark (merged into exp-002 Phase 2)\n  plan_id: exp-008\n  description: 'Blocked. MATH was merged into exp-002 as the 5th benchmark in Phase\n    2, which is blocked by answer extraction pipeline issues. Free-form symbolic math\n    answer normalization (handling equivalent forms: \"1/2\", \"0.5\", \"\\\\frac{1}{2}\",\n    \"0.500\") requires engineering work that stalls.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    status: blocked\n    math_items_collected: 0\n    answer_extraction_pipeline_status: incomplete — symbolic normalization handles\n      ~70% of MATH answers correctly, remaining 30% require regex/sympy extensions\n    reason: Merged into exp-002 Phase 2; blocked by answer normalization engineering\n  result_rationale: 'MATH benchmark presents unique data engineering challenges compared\n    to MC benchmarks (MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag) and even compared to GSM8K (which has\n    simpler numerical answers). MATH answers include symbolic expressions, fractions,\n    roots, and multiple equivalent forms that require robust normalization. The extraction\n    pipeline handles simple numerical answers (GSM8K-style) but fails on ~30% of MATH\n    items where the answer is symbolic. Building a robust sympy-based normalization\n    pipeline is estimated at 20-30 engineering hours — work that is deprioritized\n    under worst-tier constraints where the focus is on completing Phase 1 analysis\n    and writing.\n\n    Impact on paper: The absence of MATH critically undermines the within-format gradient\n    test. With only GSM8K as a free-form benchmark, the within-format gradient analysis\n    has no free-form comparator. The paper cannot distinguish whether GSM8K''s low\n    item_id% (35.4%) is due to free-form format, mathematical reasoning task type,\n    or stochastic generation. This ambiguity is a significant limitation for the \"benchmark-specific\n    fingerprints\" framing — without MATH, the paper cannot determine whether the fingerprint\n    is driven by format or task content within the free-form category.\n\n    The paper addresses this by: (a) acknowledging the limitation explicitly, (b)\n    noting that MATH''s absence prevents within-free-form gradient testing, and (c)\n    framing MATH as the highest-priority extension for future work. The \"benchmark-specific\n    fingerprints\" claim is still supported by the MC benchmarks (MMLU vs ARC have\n    different structures despite same format) and the cross-format comparison (MC\n    vs completion vs free-form), but the within-format argument is weaker.\n\n    '\n  tables: []\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: No analysis possible. The paper's cross-benchmark analysis is limited\n    to 4 benchmarks (MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag, GSM8K) spanning 3 formats (MC, completion,\n    free-form) but with only 1-2 benchmarks per format. Within-format gradient testing\n    is limited to MC (MMLU vs ARC = 2 data points). The \"benchmark-specific fingerprints\"\n    finding survives but is less compelling without a within-format comparator for\n    free-form tasks.\n","numLines":571,"startLine":1,"totalLines":571}}}
