{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017Ap1keSeyhYCQr67JXnfvC","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ACL ARR reviewer guidelines scoring rubric 2025 2026\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"ARR Reviewer Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Area Chair Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/\"},{\"title\":\"Authors Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors\"},{\"title\":\"How ARR works – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewing\"},{\"title\":\"Review Form – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform\"},{\"title\":\"aclrollingreview/cfp.md at main · acl-org/aclrollingreview\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/acl-org/aclrollingreview/blob/main/cfp.md\"},{\"title\":\"CALL FOR PAPERS – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/cfp\"},{\"title\":\"aclrollingreview/reviewing.md at main · acl-org/aclrollingreview\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/acl-org/aclrollingreview/blob/main/reviewing.md\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about the ACL ARR reviewer guidelines scoring rubric for 2025-2026:\n\n## Key Scoring Changes\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation.\n\n## Scoring Requirements\n\nThe soundness scores must be justified by the text of the review. If you give a low Soundness score without finding any major faults, this means that your review is not a faithful explanation of your recommendation, and you need to revise it.\n\nSometimes you may find the work sound, but not something that is interesting for you personally. Such preferences are more subjective and should be reflected in a separate excitement score, which is orthogonal to the assessment of the soundness of the paper.\n\n## Overall Recommendation Scale\n\nThe overall recommendation uses a specific scale:\n- 5 = Consider for Award: I think this paper could be considered for an outstanding paper award at an *ACL conference (up to top 2.5% papers).\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle: I think this paper needs substantial revisions that can be completed by the next ARR cycle.\n- 1.5 = Resubmit after next cycle: I think this paper needs substantial revisions that cannot be completed by the next ARR cycle.\n- 1 = Do not resubmit: This paper has to be fully redone, or it is not relevant to the *ACL community.\n\nFor the most comprehensive and detailed reviewer guidelines, you can visit the official ARR Reviewer Guidelines page at http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b38b24c5-912c-4a28-9ffb-691a58265a42","uuid":"15daf387-e6c8-4dcd-8b88-61127eabbe07","timestamp":"2026-05-21T13:49:50.181Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"ACL ARR reviewer guidelines scoring rubric 2025 2026","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_0179oceyn1ne2Do13izLD4eP","content":[{"title":"ARR Reviewer Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines"},{"title":"ARR Area Chair Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines"},{"title":"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/"},{"title":"Authors Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors"},{"title":"How ARR works – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewing"},{"title":"Review Form – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"},{"title":"aclrollingreview/cfp.md at main · acl-org/aclrollingreview","url":"https://github.com/acl-org/aclrollingreview/blob/main/cfp.md"},{"title":"CALL FOR PAPERS – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/cfp"},{"title":"aclrollingreview/reviewing.md at main · acl-org/aclrollingreview","url":"https://github.com/acl-org/aclrollingreview/blob/main/reviewing.md"}]},"Based on the search results, here's what I found about the ACL ARR reviewer guidelines scoring rubric for 2025-2026:\n\n## Key Scoring Changes\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation.\n\n## Scoring Requirements\n\nThe soundness scores must be justified by the text of the review. If you give a low Soundness score without finding any major faults, this means that your review is not a faithful explanation of your recommendation, and you need to revise it.\n\nSometimes you may find the work sound, but not something that is interesting for you personally. Such preferences are more subjective and should be reflected in a separate excitement score, which is orthogonal to the assessment of the soundness of the paper.\n\n## Overall Recommendation Scale\n\nThe overall recommendation uses a specific scale:\n- 5 = Consider for Award: I think this paper could be considered for an outstanding paper award at an *ACL conference (up to top 2.5% papers).\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle: I think this paper needs substantial revisions that can be completed by the next ARR cycle.\n- 1.5 = Resubmit after next cycle: I think this paper needs substantial revisions that cannot be completed by the next ARR cycle.\n- 1 = Do not resubmit: This paper has to be fully redone, or it is not relevant to the *ACL community.\n\nFor the most comprehensive and detailed reviewer guidelines, you can visit the official ARR Reviewer Guidelines page at http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines."],"durationSeconds":10.265441058999997}}
